      "famousQuote": "The world is divided into men who have wit and no religion and men who have religion and no wit.",
      "nobelPrize": null,
      "majorDiscovery": "Clinical pharmacology rules, infectious disease contagion mechanisms, early inertia theories",
      "details": "Avicenna, the legendary Persian polymath and philosopher of the Islamic Golden Age. A prodigy who memorized the Quran by age 10 and practiced medicine by 16, he authored 'The Canon of Medicine' (Al-Qanun fi al-Tibb), which remained the standard medical textbook across Europe and the Islamic world for over 500 years. He was among the first to propose that infectious diseases are carried by microscopic airborne contagion, pioneered quarantine procedures, and described surgical treatments and pharmacology.",
      "curiosityStory": "Growing up in Bukhara (modern Uzbekistan), Ibn Sina was a prodigy of staggering intellect who had memorized the entire Quran by age 10. By 16, he was treating patients, stating that medicine was 'not a difficult science like mathematics and metaphysics.' He read Aristotle's Metaphysics forty times until the words were etched into his memory, staying awake through freezing nights fueled by tea and sheer curiosity.",
      "inspirationMoment": "Appalled by the superstitious folklore, bloodletting, and guesswork that dominated medieval healing, Ibn Sina realized that the human body is an interconnected biological system that must be studied through empirical anatomy, clinical trial protocols, and pharmacological rigor.",
      "howTheyDidIt": "He authored the monumental 'Al-Qanun fi al-Tibb' ('The Canon of Medicine'), a five-volume encyclopedia that synthesized Greco-Roman, Persian, and Indian medical knowledge. He established the first rules for controlled clinical drug trials (testing remedies on animals first, using uncontaminated substances, observing repeated consistency), identified the contagious nature of airborne tuberculosis, documented psychiatric links to physical illness, and described surgical techniques.",
