      "details": "Danish physicist who created the quantum model of the atom in 1913. Bohr realized that electrons do not orbit like planets in arbitrary paths, but are restricted to discrete, quantized energy levels. When an electron jumps between levels, it emits or absorbs a photon of light at an exact frequency. He led the Copenhagen interpretation of quantum mechanics and engaged in legendary philosophical debates with Einstein over quantum indeterminacy.",
      "curiosityStory": "Growing up in Copenhagen, Niels Bohr was an athletic soccer goalkeeper and deep thinker who spent hours debating philosophy with his brother Harald. He was legendary for his halting, thoughtful speech and an insistence on examining paradoxes from every possible vantage point, once remarking: 'How wonderful that we have met with a paradox. Now we have some hope of making progress.'",
      "inspirationMoment": "Working in Manchester with Ernest Rutherford, Bohr confronted a catastrophic flaw in Rutherford's planetary model of the atom: classical physics dictated that an orbiting electron should continuously radiate energy and spiral into the nucleus in a fraction of a second, collapsing all matter. Why didn't atoms collapse?",
      "howTheyDidIt": "In 1913, Bohr combined Rutherford's nucleus with Max Planck's quantum hypothesis. He proposed that electrons inhabit discrete, stationary orbits and can never reside between them. Electrons jump between energy levels by emitting or absorbing a single photon of light with an exact frequency corresponding to the energy gap ($E = h\nu$). His model precisely predicted the Balmer spectral emission lines of hydrogen. He went on to establish the Copenhagen Interpretation of quantum mechanics and formulated the Principle of Complementarity.",
      "fascinatingImpact": "Bohr unlocked the quantum architecture of the atom, transforming atomic theory and nurturing the generation of physicists that built the modern world.",
