      "curiosityStory": "In 1951, a 23-year-old American bird-watching wunderkind, James Watson, met 35-year-old British biophysicist Francis Crick at the Cavendish Laboratory in Cambridge. Neither was an experimental crystallographer, but both shared an unyielding obsession: they believed that discovering the 3D structure of DNA was the single greatest prize in biology.",
      "inspirationMoment": "In early 1953, Watson was shown Rosalind Franklin's Photo 51 by Maurice Wilkins. 'The instant I saw the picture my mouth fell open and my pulse began to race,' Watson later wrote. The clear X-shaped diffraction pattern screamed 'helix' with specific dimensions.",
      "howTheyDidIt": "Rather than conducting wet lab experiments, Watson and Crick built 3D physical models using brass wire, tin plates, and cardboard cutouts. Watson realized that adenine (A) must pair specifically with thymine (T) via two hydrogen bonds, and guanine (G) with cytosine (C) via three hydrogen bonds. The purine-pyrimidine base pairs had identical geometric widths, allowing the sugar-phosphate backbones to wind smoothly into an antiparallel double helix. On April 25, 1953, they published a one-page paper in Nature containing the immortal line: 'It has not escaped our notice that the specific pairing we have postulated immediately suggests a possible copying mechanism for the genetic material.'",
