      "curiosityStory": "Born in Lahore and raised in Madras, Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar ('Chandra') was the nephew of Nobel laureate C.V. Raman. At age 19, after winning a scholarship, he boarded the steamship Pilsna bound for Cambridge University to pursue astrophysics.",
      "inspirationMoment": "During the long 18-day voyage across the Arabian Sea in 1930, Chandra sat on deck with pencil and paper. He asked: When a massive star exhausts its nuclear fuel, what keeps it from collapsing under its own colossal gravity?",
      "howTheyDidIt": "Astronomers believed white dwarf stars were halted by quantum electron degeneracy pressure. But Chandra combined quantum mechanics with Einstein's Special Relativity. He discovered that as a star's core compresses, electrons approach the speed of light. Because nothing can exceed light speed, degeneracy pressure has a hard mathematical ceiling: if a dying star's core exceeds 1.44 times the mass of the Sun (the Chandrasekhar Limit), gravity crushes the electrons into the protons, causing the star to collapse catastrophically into a neutron star or black hole. Sir Arthur Eddington publicly mocked his theory, but Chandra was completely vindicated, winning the 1983 Nobel Prize.",
